{
  "case_id": "2021-21-404",
  "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, this case is not moot. We acknowledge that Washington's recent enactment of SB 5890 makes it uncertain whether a decision invalidating HB 1723 will ultimately produce any financial benefit to the United States."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stewart",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: Well, first, I think the\ngeneral rule -- and this is not just with\nrespect to intergovernmental immunity. It's --\napplies to immunities from suit generally under\ndecisions like FAA versus Cooper. The general\nrule is even when Congress has clearly\nmanifested its intention to waive immunity to\nsome degree, disputes about the scope of that\nwaiver are themselves subject to the clear\nstatement requirement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: What -- what do you\nthink this statute would have to look like for\nit to mean what the State of Washington says\nthis one means?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: So the limitation --\nso your position is that if there's an otherwise\nexisting extant body of generally applicable law,\nthe Washington agency charged with enforcing\nthat law can apply that extant body of law to\nfederal facilities and that that's what 3172\nauthorizes by that language that I just quoted?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, wouldn't\nyour case be much stronger if what you just said\nhad been found to be the case by the Supreme\nCourt of Washington?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: And the authority for that under Washington law is?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, do you think it allows -- it allows a state to single out a federal facility by name?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: But the state doesn't have exclusive jurisdiction, right, except insofar as Congress may allow it to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Kagan, you asked about federal employees. Section 3172(c) says that the authorization doesn't disturb Section 8101 of Title 5, and that's the Federal Employees' Compensation Act. It's apparent on the face of 3172 that this doesn't affect federal employees. It affects only federal contract workers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a29967361ad198168098af207e6d7045f634a911a4a48708c8cd91f6d31156d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a29967361ad-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the last thing, in response to Justice Breyer's question, our complaint here is not that Washington is treated the -- treating the workers too generously."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-21-404",
      "confidence": 0.0,
